 Editing for Reliable Instruction Following in Narratives Zhao, Runcong; Cao, Chengyu; Zhu, Qinglin; Lv, Xiucheng; Shao, Shun; Gui, Lin; Xu, Ruifeng; He, Yulan\n2510.01070 Eliciting Secret Knowledge from Language Models Cywiński, Bartosz; Ryd, Emil; Wang, Rowan; Rajamanoharan, Senthooran; Nanda, Neel; Conmy, Arthur; Marks, Samuel\n2511.00180 ParaScopes: What do Language Models Activations Encode About Future Text? Pochinkov, Nicky; Volkova, Yulia; Vasileva, Anna; Chereddy, Sai V R\n2511.12381 Don't Think of the White Bear: Ironic Negation in Transformer Models Under Cognitive Load Mann, Logan; Saxena, Nayan; Tandon, Sarah; Sun, Chenhao; Toteja, Savar; Zhu, Kevin\n2601.06973 LLMs Can't Play Hangman: On the Necessity of a Private Working Memory for Language Agents Baldelli, Davide; Parviz, Ali; Zouaq, Amal; Chandar, Sarath\n2603.25187 Probing the Lack of Stable Internal Beliefs in LLMs Luo, Yifan; Xu, Kangping; Lu, Yanzhen; Yuan, Yang; Yao, Andrew Chi-Chih\n2604.00209 Do LLMs Know What Is Private Internally? Probing and Steering Contextual Privacy Norms in Large Language Model Representations Wang, Haoran; Xiong, Li; Shu, Kai\n2604.09854 Spoiler Alert: Narrative Forecasting as a Metric for Tension in LLM Storytelling Sui, Peiqi; Zhu, Yutong; Cheng, Tianyi; West, Peter; So, Richard Jean; Long, Hoyt; Holtzman, Ari\n2604.12493 Latent Planning Emerges with Scale Hanna, Michael; Ameisen, Emmanuel\n2605.10794 Can You Keep a Secret? Involuntary Information Leakage in Language Model Writing Holtzman, Ari; West, Peter\n2605.28639 The Attentional White Bear Effect in Transformer Language Models Ramnauth, Rebecca; Scassellati, Brian\n2607.23379 When Activation Oracles Learn Not to Read: Concept-Specific Blind Spots in Fine-Tuned Oracles Bersia, Tobias; Gaintseva, Tatiana\nPreprint. Under review.\nSpoiler Alert:\nNarrative Forecasting as a Metric for Tension in LLM Storytelling\nPeiqi Sui1∗, Yutong Zhu2∗, Tianyi Cheng2,\nPeter West3, Richard Jean So4, Hoyt Long2, Ari Holtzman2\n1McGill University\n2University of Chicago\n3University of British Columbia\n4Duke University\npeiqi.sui@mail.mcgill.ca, {maxzhuyt,hoytlong,aholtzman}@uchicago.edu\nAbstract\nLLMs have so far failed both to generate consistently compelling stories and to\nrecognize this failure—on the leading creative-writing benchmark (EQ-Bench),\nLLM judges rank zero-shot AI stories above New Yorker short stories, a gold stan-\ndard for literary fiction. We argue that existing rubrics overlook a key dimension\nof compelling human stories: narrative tension. We introduce the 100-Endings\nmetric, which walks through a story sentence by sentence: at each position, a\nmodel predicts how the story will end 100 times given only the text so far, and we\nmeasure tension as how often predictions fail to match the ground truth. Beyond\nthe mismatch rate, the sentence-level curve yields complementary statistics, such as\ninflection rate, a geometric measure of how frequently the curve reverses direction,\ntracking twists and revelations. Unlike rubric-based judges, 100-Endings correctly\nranks New Yorker stories far above LLM outputs. Grounded in narratological\nprinciples, we design a story-generation pipeline using structural constraints, in-\ncluding analysis of story templates, idea formulation, and narrative scaffolding. Our\npipeline significantly increases narrative tension as measured by the 100-Endings\nmetric, while maintaining performance on the EQ-Bench leaderboard.\nFigure 1: Pipeline architecture and effects. Left: The story generation pipeline creates a to-do\nlist template with a reference template (Step 0), combines it with a target story idea into a thick\nintermediate plan (Step 1), and generates the final story (Step 2). Right: Pipeline effects on shape of\ntension curves as measured by the 100-Endings metric analysis.\n1\nIntroduction\nEven the largest and strongest LLMs have trouble with creativity: AI writers struggle to avoid clichés\n(Chakrabarty et al., 2024), produce diverse plot and narrative arcs (Padmakumar & He, 2024; Xu\net al., 2025), or maintain long-range narrative coherence (Gurung & Lapata, 2025; Pham et al., 2025).\nThis failure extends to evaluation, with strong, widely-adopted model judges rating professional\nhuman-written stories below low-quality AI generations (Section 3.2). Increasing model scale does\nnot resolve these shortcomings, which indicates that core aspects of compelling stories may not\n∗Equal contribution.\n1\narXiv:2604.09854v1  [cs.CL]  10 Apr 2026\n\nPreprint. Under review.\nbe naturally understood by zero-shot LLMs. We investigate how these attributes can be explicitly\nincorporated into current LLM evaluators and writers, focusing on narrative tension as a key signpost\nfor quality in human-written stories.\nOn their own, LLMs regularly fail to recognize their lack of narrative tension in writing. Humans\noften note that LLM stories are flat and trite (Chakrabarty et al., 2025), while model judges typically\nfocus on other aspects of their writing. Most rubrics simply omit tension as an evaluation criterion,\nbut even when it is included, model evaluators tend to reward surface markers of drama like urgent\nlanguage or explicit emotional labels, rather than verifying that information is genuinely withheld\nacross the narrative arc. More fundamentally, rubric-based evaluation assumes that story quality is an\naverage over item scores, yet this is not how human readers experience fiction: readers do not consult\na checklist; they read to be immersed, to feel uncertain and compelled, to arrive at the ending slightly\ntransformed. This visceral sense of tension is immediate and holistic, and a key component of what\nmakes a story worth reading—yet no rubric item, however well-worded, gives a model judge access\nto it. Reliable measurement therefore requires an approach that operationalizes tension structurally\nrather than delegating it to a judge’s proxy.\nTo address this gap, we introduce the 100-Endings metric that directly targets narrative tension\nby design. Following insights from literary theory (Section 2.1), we operationalize tension as\nunpredictability: after every sentence of a story, a model is asked to infer 100 possible endings, and\nwe use their divergence from the ground truth ending as a measurement for tension. 100-Endings\nflags predictable stories when the model successfully anticipates their endings, while stories with\ngenuine tension would exhibit narrative recalcitrance that resists easy predictions. Unlike the baseline\nof standard evaluation practices, our 100-Endings correctly identifies the high tension of high-quality\nstories from The New Yorker, widely regarded as a gold standard of contemporary literary fiction\n(Section 3).\nUsing 100-Endings as a north star, we investigate what needs to be injected into LLMs to write\nhigh-tension stories. At a high level, the answer seems to be the complexity of inputs and structural\nconstraints. We design a generation pipeline grounded in literary theory that can take a flexible\nrange of story ideas and transform them into tension-rich stories through a thick intermediate plan: a\nbeat-level scaffold specifying narrative function, information withholding, and tension mechanics,\nderived from an analysis of professional fiction (Section 4). This pipeline closes roughly half the\ngap to professional New Yorker fiction on our metric while achieving the top score on the EQ-Bench\nCreative Writing leaderboard (Section 5). Our contributions are as follows:\n1. A new metric for narrative tension. We introduce the 100-Endings Metric, which oper-\nationalizes tension as unpredictability of story endings at the sentence level—capturing a\ncrucial dimension of story quality missed by existing rubrics (Section 3.1).\n2. Evidence that LLMs systematically cannot hold tension. Using 100-Endings, we show\nthat top LLMs produce stories with the lowest narrative tension of any corpus we evaluate,\neven as rubric-based judges rate them above professional human fiction (Section 3.2).\n3. A structured pipeline improves tension. We propose a story generation pipeline grounded\nin literary theory and show that structured constraints improve narrative tension (Section 5).\nThis work highlights the need for deliberate, literary theory-driven solutions for LLM creativity:\nscale alone has failed to capture fundamental narrative elements such as tension, while explicitly\nmodeling it creates significant and demonstrable improvements. Tension is by no means the only\nmissing element in LLM writers, and explicit, narratology-driven work will be needed to map out\nand implement the full space of key narrative elements needed for true narrative machines.\n2\nRelated Work\n2.1\nLiterary and Narrative Theories of Tension.\nNarratology has long considered tension, an affective state driven by information asymmetry and\ndelayed resolution, as a key component of storytelling. Tension is the direct result of the structural\nDo LLMs Strategically Reveal, Conceal, and Infer Information?\nA Theoretical and Empirical Analysis in The Chameleon Game\nMustafa O. Karabag\nThe University of Texas at Austin\nJan Sobotka∗\nÉcole Polytechnique Fédérale de\nLausanne\nUfuk Topcu\nThe University of Texas at Austin\nABSTRACT\nLarge language model-based (LLM-based) agents have become com-\nmon in settings that include non-cooperative parties. In such set-\ntings, agents’ decision-making needs to conceal information from\ntheir adversaries, reveal information to their cooperators, and infer\ninformation to identify the other agents’ characteristics. To investi-\ngate whether LLMs have these information control and decision-\nmaking capabilities, we make LLM agents play the language-based\nhidden-identity game, The Chameleon. In this game, a group of\nnon-chameleon agents who do not know each other aim to iden-\ntify the chameleon agent without revealing a secret. The game\nrequires the aforementioned information control capabilities both\nas a chameleon and a non-chameleon. We begin with a theoretical\nanalysis for a spectrum of strategies, from concealing to revealing,\nand provide bounds on the non-chameleons’ winning probabil-\nity. The empirical results with GPT, Gemini 2.5 Pro, Llama 3.1,\nand Qwen3 models show that while non-chameleon LLM agents\nidentify the chameleon, they fail to conceal the secret from the\nchameleon, and their winning probability is far from the levels of\neven trivial strategies. Based on these empirical results and our\ntheoretical analysis, we deduce that LLM-based agents may reveal\nexcessive information to agents of unknown identities. Interestingly,\nwe find that, when instructed to adopt an information-revealing\nlevel, this level is linearly encoded in the LLM’s internal representa-\ntions. While the instructions alone are often ineffective at making\nnon-chameleon LLMs conceal, we show that steering the internal\nrepresentations in this linear direction directly can reliably induce\nconcealing behavior.\nKEYWORDS\nLarge language models, Hidden identity games, Strategic decision-\nmaking, Information concealment\n1\nINTRODUCTION\nLLM-based agents have become common in domains including\neducation [3, 48], healthcare [22, 37], finance [47, 53], and software\ndevelopment [10, 26]. These agents need to operate in the presence\nof other autonomous agents and humans where the interactions\nare not necessarily cooperative, such as negotiation scenarios [1, 4].\nIn these interactions, LLMs need to strategically strike a balance\nbetween potentially conflicting objectives of concealing information\nfrom their adversaries, revealing information to their potential\ncooperators, and inferring the characteristics of others.\nTo investigate whether LLMs have such information control\nand decision-making capabilities, we use a language-based hidden-\nidentity board game, The Chameleon [39]. We theoretically analyze\n∗This work was completed while Jan Sobotka was with The University of Texas at\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
