{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_92","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n# Literature review: LLMs are bad at keeping obvious secrets\n\n## Scope and conclusion\n\nThe open question is **whether explicit planning reduces involuntary leakage, and whether a causally relevant secret representation explains the effect**. Existing evidence supports semantic leakage and recoverable suppressed concepts; it does not establish that the proposed outline intervention works. Secret-word concealment, forbidden plot revelations, adversarial extraction, and contextual privacy are different tasks and must remain distinguishable.\n\nSearch completed 2026-10-04. Paper-finder was tried first in diligent mode and returned 119 candidates, all assigned relevance 1; manual primary-source verification and citation chasing selected 23 PDFs. All ten supplied papers, plus three close methodological papers, were read across every three-page PDF chunk. Other papers received abstract/selected-section review. [Chunk notes](notes/reading_log.md) preserve methods, appendices and discrepancies; [paper catalog](papers/README.md) provides full titles, authors, dates, local PDFs and reading depth. This is a scoped resource review, not an exhaustive systematic review. Claims below are published findings, not experiments performed here.\n\n## Key papers supplied by the research author\n\n**Holtzman & West (2026), [Can You Keep a Secret? Involuntary Information Leakage in Language Model Writing](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10794).** The closest prior work gives writers an unrelated secret and prohibits mentioning or hinting at it. A reader then guesses the secret from generated stories. It uses 15 curated words, a 15-word COCA sample, model-selected words, five main writers, cross-model readers and short/long non-story tasks. Pairwise discrimination counterbalances story order and target word. Main-story accuracy reaches 79.3% for Llama and 78.1% for Opus; GPT's 41.9% illustrates that avoidance can invert the signal. These figures are condition/model-specific. Exact-word non-disclosure is compatible with thematic information leakage. Short stock jokes leak less than longer flexible writing; decoys partially redirect content. No causal activation analysis supports the suggested attention/entropy mechanism. Reused stories make nominal pair counts unsuitable as independent statistical trials. Code is redacted in the PDF; Appendix D provides the 30 downloaded-project input words. The decoy table conflicts with the stated offset formula. **Implication:** replicate the behavioral phenomenon with matched no-secret controls before testing outlines.\n\n**Cywiński, Ryd, Rajamanoharan & Nanda (2025), [Towards eliciting latent knowledge…](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14352).** Twenty Gemma-2-9B QLoRA Taboo models internalize single secret words through hint-only examples. Logit lens, Gemma Scope SAE readouts and black-box elicitation are compared using ten starting prompts. Top-five Pass@10 is 75% for logit lens, 55% for SAE, 70% for postgame prefill and 95% for another-model prompting. This is deliberately informative hint generation, not unrelated fiction. Single-token secrets and manually interpreted features constrain generalization. [Early code](https://github.com/EmilRyd/eliciting-secrets) exists; use the expanded implementation below.\n\n**Cywiński et al. (2025), [Eliciting Secret Knowledge from Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01070), inspected v2.** Three model organisms separate Taboo words, hidden user-gender knowledge and Base64 secret side constraints. Methods include output-only auditing, logit lens, embedding similarity, SAE descriptions/tokens, activation fuzzing, prefill and user-persona sampling. Standard Taboo input/output auditing reaches 42.1% versus 84% for logit lens; white-box superiority does not generalize to every organism. Data construction includes 300+150 Taboo examples and 43,092+5,000 side-constraint examples. Appendix J shows secret evidence concentrated in assistant-control tokens in some cases. [Official code](https://github.com/cywinski/eliciting-secret-knowledge) is cloned. **Implication:** probe position matters; neither a single readout nor a single organism establishes continuous secret activity.\n\n**Baldelli, Parviz, Zouaq & Chandar (2026), [LLMs Can't Play Hangman](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06973), v3.** Theoretical public-history limits motivate private working memory, tested using Hangman and DDXPlus diagnosis variants with branch consistency checks (50 episodes per model/method). Private-state workflows improve consistency relative to standard agents. The impossibility argument assumes unresolved privately chosen state is not persistently available outside public history; it does **not** rule out successful concealment when a secret remains in hidden prompt context, as here. [Official implementation](https://github.com/chandar-lab/Hangman) is a reference, not cloned. Private-memory architecture is pruned from this project's implementation budget.\n\n**Luo et al. (2026), [Probing the Lack of Stable Internal Beliefs in LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25187).** Twenty-questions-style branches query numeric target indices to estimate target drift. Cross-entropy plus KL training on Qwen-2.5-14B improves consistency over baseline/CE-only training; explicit targets also help. This is prompted behavioral probing, not direct hidden-state measurement. Top-20 API logprob truncation and renormalization can distort inferred beliefs. Training uses 1,001 examples and eight A800 GPUs; code release is promised in the inspected paper. **Implication:** use recall/stability controls, but do not label numeric self-report an activation probe.\n\n**Mann et al. (2025), [Don't Think of the White Bear](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.12381).** ReboundBench tests negation under semantic, syntactic and repetition loads in nine models, with token-probability diagnostics and an attention-head intervention. Effects are model/task dependent, including exceptions. Its rebound baseline differs from ordinary secret-absent comparison. The released 5,000 negation prompts and 500 multi-question rows are downloaded from [official code/data](https://github.com/cesium132dot9/Dont-Think-of-the-White-Bear). **Implication:** include mention, suppression, unrelated-suppression and neutral controls; lexical rebound is not narrative foreshadowing.\n\n**Mireshghallah et al. (2023 preprint; ICLR 2024), [Can LLMs Keep a Secret? Contextual Integrity](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17884).** ConFAIDE spans sensitivity judgments, information flows, theory-of-mind confidentiality and meeting summaries. Tier 3 has roughly 270 scenarios and Tier 4 has 20 meeting transcripts. Reported summary leakage is 39% for GPT-4 and 57% for ChatGPT; chain-of-thought prompting does not reliably solve it. Substring matching misses paraphrases (16 of 200 checked examples). [Code/data](https://github.com/skywalker023/confaide) are available but not required for the selected primary dataset. **Implication:** separate disclosure from failure to convey permitted information; refusal alone is not success.\n\n**Dong et al. (2025), [Emergent Response Planning in LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06258).** Two-layer MLP probes predict six response attributes, including length, reasoning and story-animal choice, from pre-generation activations. The study uses task-specific generated data, including TinyStories/ROCStories-derived animal settings, 60/20/20 splits and multiple seeds. Temporal decoding can be U-shaped. Greedy-generation labels and attribute predictability do not establish causal planning or predict sampled trajectories without qualification. **Implication:** compare simple probes with text-only and shuffled-label baselines, grouping related prompts across splits.\n\n**Wu, Morris & Levine (2024), [Do language models plan ahead for future tokens?](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00859).** Distinguishes useful anticipatory computation (“pre-caching”) from representations that incidentally help later prediction (“breadcrumbs”). Myopic training removes cross-position gradient incentives; synthetic and language-model comparisons show why future-token decodability alone is insufficient. Reported GPT-2 losses differ modestly (3.28 versus 3.40), unlike a much worse bigram baseline. [Implementation](https://github.com/wiwu2390/FutureGPT2-public) is available. **Implication:** causal controls are necessary; reproducing pretraining is outside this resource/compute budget.\n\n**Xie & Riedl (2024), [Creating Suspenseful Stories](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17119).** Iterative background, action/failure planning and elaboration improves human-rated suspense over direct generation and Re3: reported preferences 84.9% and 81.1%, respectively, with 30 story pairs and 90 raters. Its aim intentionally includes tension-building cues. No official implementation was identified in the inspected PDF. **Implication:** outlining can improve suspense while increasing hints; suspense preference is not a secrecy metric.\n\n## Additional evidence that changes experimental design\n\n**Ramnauth & Scassellati (2026), [Attentional White Bear](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.28639), full read.** Seventeen concepts, 986 positive/negative/hard-negative sentences and five matched prompt conditions support layerwise logistic probes, attention analysis and semantic-similarity scoring across Llama, Mistral and Gemma. Reported mean-pooled probe AUROC is about .919 versus .86 for last-token pooling. Suppressed concepts remain decodable, but pooling and layer change the result; mentioned concepts generally exceed suppressed ones. Semantic analysis excludes explicit-leak cases, making it conditional rather than total leakage. Attention/readout correlations are not causal erasure. [Code and concepts](https://github.com/rramnauth2220/representational-suppression) are local. This substantially narrows D2's novelty to plan-conditioned temporal dynamics linked to D3.\n\n**Zhao et al. (2025), [Sparse Activation Editing…](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.16505), full read.** Concise-SAE selects instruction-supportive/opposing features via keyword-aggregated contrastive activations, then optimizes edits for compliance, response rate and quality. FreeInstruct contains 1,212 narrative cases. Llama-3.1-8B instruction-following rises from .340 unedited to .860, versus .627 ICL and .787 ICV; these are instruction-adherence outcomes, not secret-leakage scores. Strong edits degrade output. Greedy one-response evaluation, evaluator dependence and SAE availability limit conclusions. [Code/data](https://github.com/Chacioc/Concise-SAE) are local; the paper states CC BY-NC 4.0 for data.\n\n**Wang, Xiong & Shu (2026), [Contextual Privacy Probing and Steering](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00209), full read.** Synthetic paired CI examples identify information-type, recipient and transmission-principle directions; compositional steering is compared with additive steering, LoRRA and representation tuning on ConFAIDE/PrivaCI. The synthetic leakage table reports 5.0% where prose/appendices report 0.5%. Crucially, Appendix H reports helpfulness/coherence/relevance all falling to 1/5 at CI coefficient 1 on 200 unrelated instructions. This is strong reason to require utility controls, not evidence that useful secrets can simply be erased. [Code](https://github.com/wang2226/CI-Steering) is local.\n\nThe remaining downloaded papers provide focused supporting context:\n\n|Paper|Method/result relevant to this project|Use and limit|\n|---|---|---|\n|[Yang et al., DOC (2022/ACL2023)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10077)|Hierarchical outlines plus passage controller; +22.5 percentage-point coherence preference over Re3|Cloned outline templates; no secrecy evaluation|\n|[Yang et al., Re3 (2022)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06774)|Recursive reprompting/revision for long stories|Rolling-context baseline; older API stack|\n|[Yao et al., Plan-and-Write (2018/AAAI2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.05701)|Static versus interleaved keyword-storyline generation|Motivates separated planning, not evidence of reduced leakage|\n|[Fan et al. (2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.04833)|303,358 WritingPrompts pairs; hierarchical generation|Test split downloaded; no hidden-secret labels|\n|[Gonen et al. (2024/NAACL2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.06518)|109 semantic-leakage cases, 13 models, human/automatic evaluation|Irrelevant prompt concepts can bias output without suppression|\n|[Hanna & Ameisen (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12493)|Qwen3 scaling; causal forward and backward licensing tests|Useful causal criterion; simple token/rhyme tasks differ from plots|\n|[Pochinkov et al., ParaScopes (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.00180)|Residual decoders recover information equivalent to 5+ future tokens|Prediction need not imply planning; avoid readout overclaim|\n|[Bersia & Gaintseva (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.23379)|Fine-tuned activation oracles develop concept-specific reporting blind spots|Failed oracle output does not establish missing representation|\n|[Karabag et al. (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.19398)|Chameleon conceal/reveal tradeoff; latent steering can alter concealment|Strategic game signal, not spontaneous prose leakage|\n|[Sui et al., Spoiler Alert (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.09854)|100 ending forecasts per sentence; scaffold improves tension|Use cheaper prefix secret-inference curves; unpredictability alone can reward incoherence|\n\n## Common methods, baselines and evaluation\n\nBehavioral studies compare secret-present/suppressed generation with neutral, mention, decoy or context controls; interpretability studies use layerwise probes, logit-lens and sparse features. Planning systems separate outline construction from drafting. Their scores are not interchangeable: disclosure rate, secret-guess accuracy, attribute AUROC, instruction adherence and suspense preference measure different outcomes.\n\nFor this project use exact/alias disclosure **and** blinded, counterbalanced semantic secret discrimination. Calibrate candidate priors on secret-absent outputs; estimate uncertainty over independent secret/premise units. Report raw below-chance accuracy rather than treating it as no information. Probe held-out contexts with generated-token and matched teacher-forced measurements. Causal edits require random/unrelated norm-matched controls, dose-response and quality/recall checks. Human judgments should audit a subset of both leakage and coherence; LLM judges may share the writer's biases.\n\n## Gaps and recommended experiments\n\nKeep only the three ranked directions in [planning.md](planning.md): **D1** matched outline/no-outline leakage; **D2** temporal decoding under those conditions; **D3** targeted interventions with retained utility. Secret-blind outlines must precede secret assignment; secret-aware plans introduce their own information channel. The existing literature does not isolate that comparison, nor demonstrate selective removal of a causal plot-secret representation during coherent prose.\n\nUse the published 30-word list plus eligible WritingPrompts premises for D1, the suppression concept library for D2 calibration, and FreeInstruct for narrative adherence controls. ReboundBench is a secondary lexical baseline. A new, validated set of counterfactual plot secrets is required before generalizing to foreshadowing; the local illustrative fixture is not a benchmark. No experiments or hypothesis-confirming results were produced during resource gathering.\nEOF\nwc -w literature_review.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_92","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n# Literature review: LLMs are bad at keeping obvious secrets\n\n## Scope and conclusion\n\nThe open question is **whether explicit planning reduces involuntary leakage, and whether a causally relevant secret representation explains the effect**. Existing evidence supports semantic leakage and recoverable suppressed concepts; it does not establish that the proposed outline intervention works. Secret-word concealment, forbidden plot revelations, adversarial extraction, and contextual privacy are different tasks and must remain distinguishable.\n\nSearch completed 2026-10-04. Paper-finder was tried first in diligent mode and returned 119 candidates, all assigned relevance 1; manual primary-source verification and citation chasing selected 23 PDFs. All ten supplied papers, plus three close methodological papers, were read across every three-page PDF chunk. Other papers received abstract/selected-section review. [Chunk notes](notes/reading_log.md) preserve methods, appendices and discrepancies; [paper catalog](papers/README.md) provides full titles, authors, dates, local PDFs and reading depth. This is a scoped resource review, not an exhaustive systematic review. Claims below are published findings, not experiments performed here.\n\n## Key papers supplied by the research author\n\n**Holtzman & West (2026), [Can You Keep a Secret? Involuntary Information Leakage in Language Model Writing](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10794).** The closest prior work gives writers an unrelated secret and prohibits mentioning or hinting at it. A reader then guesses the secret from generated stories. It uses 15 curated words, a 15-word COCA sample, model-selected words, five main writers, cross-model readers and short/long non-story tasks. Pairwise discrimination counterbalances story order and target word. Main-story accuracy reaches 79.3% for Llama and 78.1% for Opus; GPT's 41.9% illustrates that avoidance can invert the signal. These figures are condition/model-specific. Exact-word non-disclosure is compatible with thematic information leakage. Short stock jokes leak less than longer flexible writing; decoys partially redirect content. No causal activation analysis supports the suggested attention/entropy mechanism. Reused stories make nominal pair counts unsuitable as independent statistical trials. Code is redacted in the PDF; Appendix D provides the 30 downloaded-project input words. The decoy table conflicts with the stated offset formula. **Implication:** replicate the behavioral phenomenon with matched no-secret controls before testing outlines.\n\n**Cywiński, Ryd, Rajamanoharan & Nanda (2025), [Towards eliciting latent knowledge…](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14352).** Twenty Gemma-2-9B QLoRA Taboo models internalize single secret words through hint-only examples. Logit lens, Gemma Scope SAE readouts and black-box elicitation are compared using ten starting prompts. Top-five Pass@10 is 75% for logit lens, 55% for SAE, 70% for postgame prefill and 95% for another-model prompting. This is deliberately informative hint generation, not unrelated fiction. Single-token secrets and manually interpreted features constrain generalization. [Early code](https://github.com/EmilRyd/eliciting-secrets) exists; use the expanded implementation below.\n\n**Cywiński et al. (2025), [Eliciting Secret Knowledge from Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01070), inspected v2.** Three model organisms separate Taboo words, hidden user-gender knowledge and Base64 secret side constraints. Methods include output-only auditing, logit lens, embedding similarity, SAE descriptions/tokens, activation fuzzing, prefill and user-persona sampling. Standard Taboo input/output auditing reaches 42.1% versus 84% for logit lens; white-box superiority does not generalize to every organism. Data construction includes 300+150 Taboo examples and 43,092+5,000 side-constraint examples. Appendix J shows secret evidence concentrated in assistant-control tokens in some cases. [Official code](https://github.com/cywinski/eliciting-secret-knowledge) is cloned. **Implication:** probe position matters; neither a single readout nor a single organism establishes continuous secret activity.\n\n**Baldelli, Parviz, Zouaq & Chandar (2026), [LLMs Can't Play Hangman](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06973), v3.** Theoretical public-history limits motivate private working memory, tested using Hangman and DDXPlus diagnosis variants with branch consistency checks (50 episodes per model/method). Private-state workflows improve consistency relative to standard agents. The impossibility argument assumes unresolved privately chosen state is not persistently available outside public history; it does **not** rule out successful concealment when a secret remains in hidden prompt context, as here. [Official implementation](https://github.com/chandar-lab/Hangman) is a reference, not cloned. Private-memory architecture is pruned from this project's implementation budget.\n\n**Luo et al. (2026), [Probing the Lack of Stable Internal Beliefs in LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25187).** Twenty-questions-style branches query numeric target indices to estimate target drift. Cross-entropy plus KL training on Qwen-2.5-14B improves consistency over baseline/CE-only training; explicit targets also help. This is prompted behavioral probing, not direct hidden-state measurement. Top-20 API logprob truncation and renormalization can distort inferred beliefs. Training uses 1,001 examples and eight A800 GPUs; code release is promised in the inspected paper. **Implication:** use recall/stability controls, but do not label numeric self-report an activation probe.\n\n**Mann et al. (2025), [Don't Think of the White Bear](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.12381).** ReboundBench tests negation under semantic, syntactic and repetition loads in nine models, with token-probability diagnostics and an attention-head intervention. Effects are model/task dependent, including exceptions. Its rebound baseline differs from ordinary secret-absent comparison. The released 5,000 negation prompts and 500 multi-question rows are downloaded from [official code/data](https://github.com/cesium132dot9/Dont-Think-of-the-White-Bear). **Implication:** include mention, suppression, unrelated-suppression and neutral controls; lexical rebound is not narrative foreshadowing.\n\n**Mireshghallah et al. (2023 preprint; ICLR 2024), [Can LLMs Keep a Secret? Contextual Integrity](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17884).** ConFAIDE spans sensitivity judgments, information flows, theory-of-mind confidentiality and meeting summaries. Tier 3 has roughly 270 scenarios and Tier 4 has 20 meeting transcripts. Reported summary leakage is 39% for GPT-4 and 57% for ChatGPT; chain-of-thought prompting does not reliably solve it. Substring matching misses paraphrases (16 of 200 checked examples). [Code/data](https://github.com/skywalker023/confaide) are available but not required for the selected primary dataset. **Implication:** separate disclosure from failure to convey permitted information; refusal alone is not success.\n\n**Dong et al. (2025), [Emergent Response Planning in LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06258).** Two-layer MLP probes predict six response attributes, including length, reasoning and story-animal choice, from pre-generation activations. The study uses task-specific generated data, including TinyStories/ROCStories-derived animal settings, 60/20/20 splits and multiple seeds. Temporal decoding can be U-shaped. Greedy-generation labels and attribute predictability do not establish causal planning or predict sampled trajectories without qualification. **Implication:** compare simple probes with text-only and shuffled-label baselines, grouping related prompts across splits.\n\n**Wu, Morris & Levine (2024), [Do language models plan ahead for future tokens?](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00859).** Distinguishes useful anticipatory computation (“pre-caching”) from representations that incidentally help later prediction (“breadcrumbs”). Myopic training removes cross-position gradient incentives; synthetic and language-model comparisons show why future-token decodability alone is insufficient. Reported GPT-2 losses differ modestly (3.28 versus 3.40), unlike a much worse bigram baseline. [Implementation](https://github.com/wiwu2390/FutureGPT2-public) is available. **Implication:** causal controls are necessary; reproducing pretraining is outside this resource/compute budget.\n\n**Xie & Riedl (2024), [Creating Suspenseful Stories](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17119).** Iterative background, action/failure planning and elaboration improves human-rated suspense over direct generation and Re3: reported preferences 84.9% and 81.1%, respectively, with 30 story pairs and 90 raters. Its aim intentionally includes tension-building cues. No official implementation was identified in the inspected PDF. **Implication:** outlining can improve suspense while increasing hints; suspense preference is not a secrecy metric.\n\n## Additional evidence that changes experimental design\n\n**Ramnauth & Scassellati (2026), [Attentional White Bear](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.28639), full read.** Seventeen concepts, 986 positive/negative/hard-negative sentences and five matched prompt conditions support layerwise logistic probes, attention analysis and semantic-similarity scoring across Llama, Mistral and Gemma. Reported mean-pooled probe AUROC is about .919 versus .86 for last-token pooling. Suppressed concepts remain decodable, but pooling and layer change the result; mentioned concepts generally exceed suppressed ones. Semantic analysis excludes explicit-leak cases, making it conditional rather than total leakage. Attention/readout correlations are not causal erasure. [Code and concepts](https://github.com/rramnauth2220/representational-suppression) are local. This substantially narrows D2's novelty to plan-conditioned temporal dynamics linked to D3.\n\n**Zhao et al. (2025), [Sparse Activation Editing…](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.16505), full read.** Concise-SAE selects instruction-supportive/opposing features via keyword-aggregated contrastive activations, then optimizes edits for compliance, response rate and quality. FreeInstruct contains 1,212 narrative cases. Llama-3.1-8B instruction-following rises from .340 unedited to .860, versus .627 ICL and .787 ICV; these are instruction-adherence outcomes, not secret-leakage scores. Strong edits degrade output. Greedy one-response evaluation, evaluator dependence and SAE availability limit conclusions. [Code/data](https://github.com/Chacioc/Concise-SAE) are local; the paper states CC BY-NC 4.0 for data.\n\n**Wang, Xiong & Shu (2026), [Contextual Privacy Probing and Steering](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00209), full read.** Synthetic paired CI examples identify information-type, recipient and transmission-principle directions; compositional steering is compared with additive steering, LoRRA and representation tuning on ConFAIDE/PrivaCI. The synthetic leakage table reports 5.0% where prose/appendices report 0.5%. Crucially, Appendix H reports helpfulness/coherence/relevance all falling to 1/5 at CI coefficient 1 on 200 unrelated instructions. This is strong reason to require utility controls, not evidence that useful secrets can simply be erased. [Code](https://github.com/wang2226/CI-Steering) is local.\n\nThe remaining downloaded papers provide focused supporting context:\n\n|Paper|Method/result relevant to this project|Use and limit|\n|---|---|---|\n|[Yang et al., DOC (2022/ACL2023)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10077)|Hierarchical outlines plus passage controller; +22.5 percentage-point coherence preference over Re3|Cloned outline templates; no secrecy evaluation|\n|[Yang et al., Re3 (2022)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06774)|Recursive reprompting/revision for long stories|Rolling-context baseline; older API stack|\n|[Yao et al., Plan-and-Write (2018/AAAI2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.05701)|Static versus interleaved keyword-storyline generation|Motivates separated planning, not evidence of reduced leakage|\n|[Fan et al. (2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.04833)|303,358 WritingPrompts pairs; hierarchical generation|Test split downloaded; no hidden-secret labels|\n|[Gonen et al. (2024/NAACL2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.06518)|109 semantic-leakage cases, 13 models, human/automatic evaluation|Irrelevant prompt concepts can bias output without suppression|\n|[Hanna & Ameisen (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12493)|Qwen3 scaling; causal forward and backward licensing tests|Useful causal criterion; simple token/rhyme tasks differ from plots|\n|[Pochinkov et al., ParaScopes (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.00180)|Residual decoders recover information equivalent to 5+ future tokens|Prediction need not imply planning; avoid readout overclaim|\n|[Bersia & Gaintseva (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.23379)|Fine-tuned activation oracles develop concept-specific reporting blind spots|Failed oracle output does not establish missing representation|\n|[Karabag et al. (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.19398)|Chameleon conceal/reveal tradeoff; latent steering can alter concealment|Strategic game signal, not spontaneous prose leakage|\n|[Sui et al., Spoiler Alert (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.09854)|100 ending forecasts per sentence; scaffold improves tension|Use cheaper prefix secret-inference curves; unpredictability alone can reward incoherence|\n\n## Common methods, baselines and evaluation\n\nBehavioral studies compare secret-present/suppressed generation with neutral, mention, decoy or context controls; interpretability studies use layerwise probes, logit-lens and sparse features. Planning systems separate outline construction from drafting. Their scores are not interchangeable: disclosure rate, secret-guess accuracy, attribute AUROC, instruction adherence and suspense preference measure different outcomes.\n\nFor this project use exact/alias disclosure **and** blinded, counterbalanced semantic secret discrimination. Calibrate candidate priors on secret-absent outputs; estimate uncertainty over independent secret/premise units. Report raw below-chance accuracy rather than treating it as no information. Probe held-out contexts with generated-token and matched teacher-forced measurements. Causal edits require random/unrelated norm-matched controls, dose-response and quality/recall checks. Human judgments should audit a subset of both leakage and coherence; LLM judges may share the writer's biases.\n\n## Gaps and recommended experiments\n\nKeep only the three ranked directions in [planning.md](planning.md): **D1** matched outline/no-outline leakage; **D2** temporal decoding under those conditions; **D3** targeted interventions with retained utility. Secret-blind outlines must precede secret assignment; secret-aware plans introduce their own information channel. The existing literature does not isolate that comparison, nor demonstrate selective removal of a causal plot-secret representation during coherent prose.\n\nUse the published 30-word list plus eligible WritingPrompts premises for D1, the suppression concept library for D2 calibration, and FreeInstruct for narrative adherence controls. ReboundBench is a secondary lexical baseline. A new, validated set of counterfactual plot secrets is required before generalizing to foreshadowing; the local illustrative fixture is not a benchmark. No experiments or hypothesis-confirming results were produced during resource gathering.\nEOF\nwc -w literature_review.md\"","aggregated_output":"1767 literature_review.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_93","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; test -d .git\ncat > scripts/build_resource_catalog.py <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nassert (Path.cwd()/'.git').exists()\npapers=json.loads(Path('notes/papers_catalog.json').read_text());data=json.loads(Path('datasets/manifest.json').read_text());repos=json.loads(Path('code/manifest.json').read_text())\nuses={'2605.10794':'Closest leakage task; word list and judge design','2505.14352':'Early Taboo/logit-lens baseline','2510.01070':'Expanded secret readout methods','2601.06973':'Private-memory assumptions and recall control','2603.25187':'Belief-drift caveat; behavioral probes','2511.12381':'Negation/load controls; ReboundBench','2310.17884':'Contextual disclosure and utility','2502.06258':'Pre-response and temporal decoding','2404.00859':'Decodability versus causal planning','2402.17119':'Suspense planning; hints may increase','2605.28639':'Suppressed concept probes and data','2604.00209':'Steering code; utility collapse caution','2505.16505':'SAE editing; FreeInstruct data','2212.10077':'Hierarchical outline implementation','2210.06774':'Recursive reprompting baseline','1811.05701':'Static versus interleaved plans','1805.04833':'WritingPrompts source','2408.06518':'Semantic prompt leakage','2604.12493':'Causal planning criterion','2511.00180':'Future-context readout limits','2607.23379':'Activation oracle blind spots','2501.19398':'Strategic conceal/reveal comparison','2604.09854':'Prefix forecasts and tension'}\nlines=['# Resources catalog','', 'Resource gathering completed on 2026-10-04: **23 original papers, four downloaded source datasets (five raw files), five cloned repositories**. The project has a fresh uv environment and pinned download manifests. This handoff prepares experiments; it contains no model-run results.','', 'Start with [literature_review.md](literature_review.md), then [planning.md](planning.md). The implementation budget is fixed at D1 outline comparisons, D2 temporal decoding, and D3 causal interventions with quality controls.','', '## Papers','', '|Title|Authors|Year (first arXiv)|Local file|Use|','|---|---|---|---|---|']\nfor r in papers:\n lines.append(f\\\"|[{r['title']}](https://arxiv.org/abs/{r['id']})|{'; '.join(r['authors'])}|{r['date'][0][:4]}|[{r['id']}]({r['file']})|{uses[r['id']]}|\\\")\nlines+=['','See [papers/README.md](papers/README.md) for reading scope, [notes/papers_catalog.json](notes/papers_catalog.json) for hashes/page counts, and [notes/reading_log.md](notes/reading_log.md) for sequential chunk notes. All ten supplied papers and three additional close papers were read in full. Other papers were screened by abstract with selected-section consultation where needed.','', '## Datasets','', '|Resource|Local location|Downloaded extent|Primary use|','|---|---|---|---|','|Suppression concepts|\"'`datasets/suppression_concepts/`|17 concepts; 986 labeled probe sentences plus aliases/contexts|D2/D3 contrast construction|'\"','|FreeInstruct|\"'`datasets/freeinstruct/`|1,212 narrative cases|Narrative adherence/utility controls|'\"','|ReboundBench released prompts|\"'`datasets/reboundbench/`|5,000 negation + 500 multi-question rows|D1/D2 lexical suppression controls|'\"','|WritingPrompts test|\"'`datasets/writingprompts/`|15,138 pairs, 5,478 distinct premises|D1 source premises|'\"','',f\\\"Raw files total {sum(x['bytes'] for x in data):,} bytes. [Download and loading instructions](datasets/README.md), [pinned URLs and SHA-256](datasets/manifest.json), [structural validation](datasets/validation.json). Raw data are excluded from Git; small actual samples remain available. Concept data are CC BY 4.0; FreeInstruct paper states CC BY-NC 4.0; ReboundBench license was not located; WritingPrompts mirror labels MIT without resolving original Reddit rights.\\\",'','Two **derived resources are not counted as downloaded datasets**: [30 published secret words](datasets/derived/secret_words.json) and [one illustrative plot-secret fixture](datasets/derived/plot_secret_fixture.json). The latter still requires construction/validation of a real counterfactual plot benchmark.','', '## Code','', '|Repository|Pinned commit|Location|Purpose|','|---|---|---|---|']\nrepouses=['Secret readouts/logit lens/SAEs','Matched suppression probes','Sparse instruction feature editing','Compositional residual steering and utility','Hierarchical outline structures']\nfor r,u in zip(repos,repouses):lines.append(f\\\"|[{r['repo']}]({r['url']})|\"'`{r['\"'commit']}\"'`|`{r['\"'path']}/\"'`|{u}|\")\nlines+=['\"'','[code/README.md](code/README.md) records entry points, dependencies, licenses, adaptation issues and execution limits. All READMEs inspected; ten selected modules pass AST syntax parsing. No upstream model example was executed. Sparse clones omit large results/outputs/figures, and nested repositories are excluded from the outer Git repository. Recreate with \"'`scripts/clone_resources.py`; repository pins are also in [code/manifest.json](code/manifest.json).'\"','', '## Search strategy and selection','', 'Keywords: involuntary information leakage; secret-word story writing; semantic leakage; ironic suppression/white bear; explicit narrative outlines; latent response planning; activation probing; sparse activation editing; contextual integrity; premature foreshadowing.','', 'Paper-finder was attempted first. Its missing httpx dependency was installed in the fresh environment, and the diligent retry succeeded: 119 candidates, all relevance=1. Results are preserved in \"'`logs/paper_finder_retry.json` and `paper_search_results/`; [screening ledger](notes/search_screening.json) records selections. Thirteen direct service candidates were downloaded, with additional supplied papers and citation-chased methods making 23 unique PDFs. Lower-priority adjacent games, storytelling UX, multimodal generation and other methods were not all downloaded; downloading all 119 would defeat the stated relevance and three-direction scope. This is an explicitly scoped selection, not a claim that every returned paper was read or downloaded.'\"','', 'Primary-source arXiv pages/PDFs were used to resolve every supplied unknown title and author list; official GitHub links supplied implementations. Hugging Face was checked first for narrative datasets, then paper-linked repositories for more direct probes. Story Cloze was considered but its labels do not measure forbidden foreshadowing. No staged local resources or explicit repository references were provided.','', '## Challenges and remaining limitations','', '- The closest leakage paper redacts its code URL. Its published prompts/word list are usable, but this is not an official-code reproduction. The Luo paper promises code release; a verified public implementation was not identified.','- Latest downloaded arXiv versions include differences and internal inconsistencies: decoy offset in 2605.10794; synthetic leakage decimal in 2604.00209; model naming between 2605.28639 and its repository. Hashes and chunk notes preserve the inspected evidence.','- Suppression probing is already prior art. Novelty rests on controlled outline effects and causal tests that retain writing utility; a decoder becoming inaccurate does not prove secret removal.','- WritingPrompts repeats premises; FreeInstruct has one duplicate story. Split by context/template families. Published arbitrary-word results cannot be relabeled as evidence about future plot endings.','- Five source downloads parsed successfully, but this is structural validation, not validation of benchmark labels or model quality. Some licenses need source-specific interpretation before redistribution.','- No checkpoint or SAE weights were downloaded and no inference API was called. An RTX A6000 (49,140 MiB) was visible, but model access, compatible runtime packages and actual free memory must be checked by the experiment runner. Upstream frozen dependencies conflict; install only the selected runtime components. DOC relies on legacy interfaces and should supply templates rather than a drop-in runner.','', '## Concrete next steps','', '1. Re-run \"'`uv sync` and `.venv/bin/python scripts/validate_resources.py` from the root. Use the existing project `.venv`; no conda or shared environment.'\"','2. Build grouped input/split manifests using the 30 words and eligible WritingPrompts premises; construct outlines before secret assignment. Pilot the six D1 conditions, separating word secrecy from the later plot-secret extension.','3. Add blinded/counterbalanced semantic inference, exact-alias checks, no-secret calibration, quality and recall controls. Size the main study from the paired pilot variance, not reused judge-pair counts.','4. Adapt the suppression repository for generated-token and identical-continuation D2 readouts. Only then apply validated D3 interventions with random/unrelated controls, cache accounting and quality limits. Full protocol and pruned alternatives are in planning.md.','', '## Environment and reproducibility','', '\"'`pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` capture the resource environment (Python>=3.10; actual interpreter recorded by final validation). Installed tooling: requests/httpx, pypdf, PyMuPDF, Pillow and pyarrow. Download helpers live under `scripts/`; PDF chunking uses `.codex/skills/paper-finder/scripts/pdf_chunker.py`. Original PDFs stay in papers/, data in datasets/, and clones in code/. Auxiliary extracted text and page images are reproducible and Git-ignored.'\"','', 'Final validation details are in [notes/resource_validation.json](notes/resource_validation.json). \"'`.resource_finder_complete` is created only after those checks pass. STATE.md changes are confined to the resource_finder notes block.'\"']\nPath('resources.md').write_text('\\\\n'.join(lines)+'\\\\n')\nPY\n.venv/bin/python scripts/build_resource_catalog.py\n.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('scripts/catalog_papers.py');s=p.read_text();s=s[:s.index(\\\"p=Path('STATE.md')\\\")];s=s.replace(\\\"'2605.28639','2604.00209'\\\",\\\"'2605.28639','2604.00209','2505.16505'\\\");s=s.replace('abstract and selected methods; not full text','abstract screening; selected sections where relevant');p.write_text(s)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
